; ======================================================================
;  mmu.pi3 - the Cortex-A53 MMU and caches on the Raspberry Pi 3,
;            and the reason the Pi 3 monitor cannot be written without it
; ======================================================================
;
;  ---- WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, AND WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE --------
;
;  The Pi 4's mmu.pi4 was staged: the monitor ran perfectly well with
;  translation off for months and the MMU was brought up to make the
;  machine FASTER. On this board the MMU is not an optimisation. It is
;  the thing that makes the shared core RUNNABLE, and that is a different
;  argument which is worth stating before anyone decides to skip a stage.
;
;  With SCTLR_ELx.M clear, ARMv8-A treats EVERY data access as
;  Device-nGnRnE regardless of anything else, and an unaligned access to
;  Device memory is an Alignment fault - not a slow path, a fault, and
;  not one that SCTLR.A can turn off, because SCTLR.A only governs Normal
;  memory. Anvil's shared core and the shared filesystems read packed
;  on-disk structures at whatever byte offset the format puts them:
;  Anvil/Storage/fat32.pbi and exfat.pbi walk directory entries,
;  Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi walks message blocks, Anvil/Core/settings.pbi
;  walks text. Any one of those landing odd is a data abort.
;
;  The existing Pi 3 code gets away with translation off because it is
;  careful, small and hand-written against that constraint - the loader,
;  the updater and the early diagnostics never make an unaligned access.
;  The shared core makes no such promise and should not have to: it is
;  the same source that runs on two boards where the MMU is already on.
;
;  So: translation on, caches on, before the prompt. The alternative is
;  a monitor that faults the first time somebody types `sha256sum`.
;
;  ---- ON RISK, CALIBRATED --------------------------------------------
;
;  Nothing here can brick a Pi 3. A bad table hangs the board or resets
;  it, and the cost is a power cycle. There is no EEPROM on this part -
;  a Pi 3 boots from the card, so the very worst case is rewriting the
;  card on a PC. The care taken below is about DIAGNOSABILITY, which is
;  a genuinely different objective: a hang with the MMU half on gives no
;  console and no clue, so everything that CAN be checked is checked
;  BEFORE the enabling instruction, and MmuBuildTables() is a separate
;  procedure from MmuEnable() precisely so its output can be dumped and
;  read while the board is still in no danger at all.
;
;  This board has a second safety net the Pi 4 did not have at the same
;  stage: this is firmware-loaded kernel8.img; no slot selector or trial
;  watchdog wraps it. A failed table setup is reported over UART and the
;  processor parks for a manual reset.
;
;  ---- THE ORDER, AND IT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART OF THIS FILE --------
;
;  Firmware property traffic is supported both before and after MmuUp(),
;  but post-MMU transactions have a stricter buffer contract.
;
;  Pi3MailboxContext() accepts the cache-off cold path, or (with M/C on)
;  only a complete buffer span contained in one of the exact ranges
;  registered through MmuAddNc() before table construction. The buffer
;  must remain 16-byte aligned, primary-core resident ARM RAM. The MMU
;  maps those registered 2 MiB blocks Normal Non-Cacheable, which keeps
;  VideoCore property reads/writes coherent without disabling translation
;  or relying on cache-maintenance code. Unregistered/cached buffers and
;  secondary cores are refused.
;
;  Cold boot still performs the clock, memory-extent, board revision,
;  framebuffer allocation and DTB-related property work before MmuUp(),
;  then registers the property and framebuffer ranges. Later operations
;  such as measured clock queries and firmware GPIO state changes can use
;  those same registered property buffers after MmuUp(). Mailbox timeouts
;  retain the existing outstanding-buffer quarantine: an uncompleted
;  firmware request is never retried or reused.
;
;  ---- THE MAP --------------------------------------------------------
;
;  The whole of this board's address space of interest fits in two
;  gigabytes, which is why the level-1 table has FOUR entries where the
;  Pi 4's has sixty-four. From arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2837.dtsi
;  lines 8-10 in the pinned Raspberry Pi Linux reference:
;
;      ranges = <0x7e000000  0x3f000000  0x1000000>,
;               <0x40000000  0x40000000  0x00001000>;
;      dma-ranges = <0xc0000000  0x00000000  0x3f000000>;
;
;  which says: the legacy $7E000000 peripheral bus window is at physical
;  $3F000000 and is 16 MB; the ARM-local window is at $40000000 and is
;  4 KB; and the DRAM the VideoCore can see runs from 0 for $3F000000
;  bytes. So:
;
;    $00000000..$3EFFFFFF   DRAM, Normal Write-Back cacheable, minus any
;                           range MmuAddNc() has asked to keep coherent
;    $3F000000..$3FFFFFFF   the BCM2837 peripherals, Device-nGnRnE
;    $40000000..$401FFFFF   the ARM-local peripherals, Device-nGnRnE.
;                           The window is only 4 KB; this is the 2 MB
;                           block containing it, because a level-2 block
;                           is the finest thing these tables express.
;                           RaspberryPi3/Board/hw_addr.pi3 refuses above
;                           $40000FFF and says so - the guard is tighter
;                           than the tables here, deliberately, and that
;                           asymmetry is written down in both files.
;    everything else        FAULT
;
;  THERE IS NO SECOND RUN OF DRAM. On the Pi 4 the 64 MB the peripheral
;  aperture displaces reappears above four gigabytes, and a monitor that
;  invited an operator to type that address cost a board its power on
;  2026-09-18 (the vault's "Memory size and the unmapped second run").
;  The BCM2837 has no such run: 1 GB is fitted, the top 16 MB of it is
;  under the peripheral window, and that memory is simply not reachable
;  by the ARM. So the honest top of DRAM here is $3EFFFFFF and the guard
;  says ABSENT above it rather than inviting anybody anywhere.
;
;  ---- GEOMETRY -------------------------------------------------------
;
;  4 KB granule, 32-bit input address (T0SZ = 32), so the walk starts at
;  level 1 with index bits [31:30]: FOUR level-1 descriptors, each
;  covering 1 GiB. Two of them are split by level-2 tables of 512
;  entries at 2 MiB each. Three 4 KiB tables in all, 12,288 bytes.
;
;  The Pi 4 uses a 36-bit input address because it has to reach a PCIe
;  outbound window at $6_0000_0000. Nothing on this board is above
;  $40001000, so a 32-bit input address is not a saving - it is the
;  truth, and it means an address above 4 GiB is refused by the
;  translation regime itself rather than by a slot left at FAULT.
;
;  ---- WHAT TURNING THE CACHES ON CHANGES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT -------
;
;  ON THIS BOARD, LESS THAN ON THE PI 4, AND THAT IS WORTH KNOWING
;  BEFORE ANYBODY GOES LOOKING FOR TROUBLE. The Pi 4's note warns that
;  every driver that DMAs - xhci, genet, usbmsc, the mailbox - has
;  cache-maintenance calls that are no-ops until DRAM is cacheable and
;  have never been exercised. The equivalent audit for this board, done
;  before this file was written:
;
;    * SDHOST IS PROGRAMMED I/O. RaspberryPi3/Lib/sdhost.pbi's
;      p3sdTransfer() moves the block a word at a time through the data
;      FIFO at register $40 - PeekL/PokeL on the caller's buffer and
;      p3sdRead/p3sdWrite on the register. There is no bus master, so
;      there is no coherency question, so the whole storage path is safe
;      with caches on with no maintenance at all. This is the single
;      biggest reason M1 can turn the caches on at the same time as the
;      MMU rather than staging them.
;    * THE MAILBOX IS the one bus master in the M1 image, and it is
;      handled by the ORDER above rather than by maintenance.
;    * THE USB HOST AND THE ETHERNET ADAPTER do DMA, and they are not in
;      the M1 image at all. Whoever brings them in owns that audit, and
;      MmuAddNc() below is the mechanism, the same one the Pi 4 uses for
;      its framebuffer.
;
;  MmuAddNc(lo, hi) marks a range to be mapped Normal NON-CACHEABLE
;  instead of Write-Back, at 2 MiB granularity, in either of the split
;  gigabytes. It is how a buffer shared with a bus master stays coherent
;  without a single maintenance call - which is strictly better than
;  maintenance nobody has ever run. Call it BEFORE MmuBuildTables().
;
;  ---- DEPENDENCIES ---------------------------------------------------
;
;  NONE. House rule 10: a library never includes another library; the
;  main file lists what it needs. This file reads and writes system
;  registers and plain memory and calls nothing.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuCurrentEl() / MmuEl() - what level is this actually running at.
;
;  READ, NEVER ASSUMED. The Pi 3's firmware normally hands off at EL2,
;  and the loader's branch (Pi3UpdateEnter in
;  RaspberryPi3/Board/update_boot_support.pbi) is a plain `br` that
;  changes nothing - so the monitor arrives wherever the firmware put
;  the loader. "Normally" is not a contract: config.txt can ask for EL3,
;  and a board whose banner asserted a level it had not read is the exact
;  defect forum 575 was about.
;
;  CurrentEL holds the level in bits 3:2, so the raw read is 4, 8 or 12
;  and MmuEl() shifts it down to 1, 2 or 3.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuCurrentEl()
  ASM
    mrs x0, currentel
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuEl()
  ProcedureReturn (MmuCurrentEl() >> 2) & 3
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuAtEl3()
  If MmuCurrentEl() = 12
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The SCTLR readers. One per level, because a system register name is
;  part of the instruction in this assembler and cannot be an operand -
;  which is also why MmuEnable() below carries two near-identical
;  sequences rather than one helper.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuSctlrEl2()
  ASM
    mrs x0, sctlr_el2
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuSctlrEl3()
  ASM
    mrs x0, sctlr_el3
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuSctlr() - the one the processor is actually using.
;
;  `cache status`, HwMmuState() and MmuEnabled() are all asking "is
;  translation on for the code running right now", so they must read the
;  register of the CURRENT level. Reading SCTLR_EL2 at EL3 is legal and
;  returns a register nothing is using, which would be a confident wrong
;  answer of the worst kind.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuSctlr()
  If MmuAtEl3() <> 0
    ProcedureReturn MmuSctlrEl3()
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MmuSctlrEl2()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The attribute constants. Every one cited, and every one identical to
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4's - these are architecture, not chip, and two
;  boards disagreeing about what a Normal Write-Back descriptor looks
;  like would be a bug in one of them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; MAIR. One byte per index, low index first:
;   0  Device-nGnRnE   $00
;   1  Device-nGnRE    $04
;   2  Device-GRE      $0C
;   3  Normal NC       $44
;   4  Normal WB       $FF
; -> $FF_440C_0400. Indices 0, 3 and 4 are used below; the other two are
; carried because the register is written whole.
#MMU3_MAIR = $FF440C0400

; TCR, for a 32-bit input address at EL2 or EL3 (the two registers have
; the same field layout, both being the one-VA-range no-VHE format):
;
;   RSVD          (1<<31)|(1<<23)   $80800000
;   PS = 0 (32-bit output address)  $00000000   the BCM2837 has no
;                                               physical address above
;                                               $40001000, so claiming
;                                               36 bits would describe
;                                               space that is not there
;   TG0 = 4 KB    (0<<14)           $00000000
;   SH0 = inner   (3<<12)           $00003000
;   ORGN0 = WBWA  (1<<10)           $00000400
;   IRGN0 = WBWA  (1<<8)            $00000100
;   T0SZ = 64-32                    $00000020
;                                   ----------
;                                   $80803520
;
; The SH0/IRGN0/ORGN0 fields describe the TABLE WALKER's own accesses,
; not the memory being mapped. They say cacheable here and that is
; correct and not an inconsistency.
#MMU3_TCR = $80803520

; A 2 MiB or 1 GiB BLOCK descriptor, Device-nGnRnE:
;   PTE_TYPE_BLOCK   (1<<0)                  $001
;   MEMTYPE index 0                          $000
;   NON_SHARE        (0<<8)                  $000
;   AF, the access flag (1<<10)              $400
;
; THE ACCESS FLAG IS NOT OPTIONAL. With AF clear the very first access
; through the descriptor takes an Access Flag fault. It is one bit, it
; is easy to leave out, and leaving it out produces a board that dies on
; the instruction after the enable with nothing on the console.
#MMU3_ATTR_DEVICE = $401

; Normal Inner/Outer Non-Cacheable - MAIR index 3, inner shareable:
;   BLOCK $001 | (3<<2) $00C | INNER_SHARE $300 | AF $400
#MMU3_ATTR_NC = $70D

; Normal Inner/Outer Write-Back cacheable - MAIR index 4, inner
; shareable:
;   BLOCK $001 | (4<<2) $010 | INNER_SHARE $300 | AF $400
#MMU3_ATTR_WB = $711

; What ordinary DRAM gets. Written as a literal rather than as
; #MMU3_ATTR_WB because this language refuses a constant declared from
; another constant in its initialiser - the same trap
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 records at the same line. The only two values
; this may ever take are $711 (Write-Back) and $70D (Non-Cacheable), and
; it is one constant so that a bad result is one edit to revert.
#MMU3_DRAM_ATTR = $711

; A TABLE descriptor is the next table's address with the low two bits
; set. No hierarchical permission bits, so a table imposes no extra
; restriction on what it points at.
#MMU3_DESC_TABLE = 3

; An all-zero descriptor faults. The name exists so the loop below says
; what it means rather than storing a bare zero.
#MMU3_DESC_FAULT = 0

; SCTLR bits. M is bit 0, C (D-cache) is bit 2, I (I-cache) is bit 12.
;
; WITH C CLEAR, ARMv8-A TREATS EVERY NORMAL ACCESS AS NON-CACHEABLE
; WHATEVER THE DESCRIPTOR SAYS. So #MMU3_SCTLR_M on its own gives
; translation with no caching even though #MMU3_DRAM_ATTR is the
; Write-Back descriptor; M|C|I is what makes the attribute take effect.
; Both are offered because the first bring-up on silicon should be able
; to separate "the tables are wrong" from "the caches broke something",
; and those are different questions with different answers.
#MMU3_SCTLR_M   = 1
#MMU3_SCTLR_C   = $4
#MMU3_SCTLR_I   = $1000
#MMU3_SCTLR_MCI = $1005

; Geometry.
#MMU3_L1_ENTRIES  = 4        ; 32-bit input address, index bits [31:30]
#MMU3_L2_ENTRIES  = 512      ; a full 4 KiB table, 9 index bits
#MMU3_TABLE_BYTES = 12288    ; three 4 KiB tables: L1, L2 for slot 0,
                             ; L2 for slot 1

; The two boundaries inside the low gigabyte, both from bcm2837.dtsi:8.
; $3F000000 is where DRAM stops being reachable by the ARM and the
; peripheral aperture begins; it is 2 MiB aligned, so a level-2 table
; splits it exactly with no remainder to argue about.
#MMU3_PERIPH_BASE = $3F000000
#MMU3_PERIPH_TOP  = $3FFFFFFF

; The ARM-local window. The hardware window is 4 KB
; ($40000000..$40000FFF); #MMU3_LOCAL_BLOCKS is how many 2 MiB blocks of
; slot 1 are mapped Device to contain it, which is one. Everything above
; that in slot 1 is FAULT.
#MMU3_LOCAL_BASE   = $40000000
#MMU3_LOCAL_TOP    = $40000FFF
#MMU3_LOCAL_BLOCKS = 1

; Where the tables live. $1F10000 is 64 KiB aligned, above the monitor's
; stack top ($1F00000, from tools/pi3_slot.py's STACK_ADDRESS) and below
; the loader's handoff page ($1FF0000, #PI3_UPDATE_HANDOFF) - a gap this
; board's slot contract leaves unused by construction. It is a constant
; rather than a BSS array because a BSS array is not 4 KiB aligned and
; hunting alignment at run time inside the one procedure that must be
; inspectable is the wrong kind of clever.
#MMU3_TABLE_BASE = $1F10000

; MmuBuildTables() refusals. Negative and distinct, because "no tables"
; and "tables somewhere useless" are different bugs and one -1 would
; merge them.
#MMU3_ERR_NULL  = -1
#MMU3_ERR_ALIGN = -2
#MMU3_ERR_LOW   = -3
#MMU3_ERR_HIGH  = -4

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The values MmuEnable() loads, written by MmuBuildTables() so that the
;  enable sequence's ASM block contains nothing but the switch itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global mmu3_ttbr.i
Global mmu3_tcr.i
Global mmu3_mair.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The non-cacheable ranges. Four is enough for every buffer this board
;  is ever likely to share with a bus master at once (the mailbox
;  property buffer, a framebuffer, and two spare for the USB and
;  Ethernet lanes) and a fixed array cannot fail to allocate.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MMU3_NC_MAX = 4
Global Dim mmu3_nc_lo.i[#MMU3_NC_MAX]
Global Dim mmu3_nc_hi.i[#MMU3_NC_MAX]
Global mmu3_ncN.i
Global mmu3_nc_ready.i

Procedure MmuClearNc()
  If mmu3_nc_ready <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  mmu3_ncN = 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuAddNc(lo, hi) - keep this range out of the caches.
;
;  Rounded OUT to 2 MiB block boundaries by mmu3_BlockNc() below, never
;  in: a buffer half of which is cached is worse than one that is wholly
;  cached, because the failure then depends on where in the buffer the
;  bus master happened to look.
;
;  Call before MmuBuildTables(). Adding a range afterwards changes
;  nothing, and this says so by refusing silently rather than by
;  pretending - MmuNcCount() is how a caller checks it took.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MmuAddNc(lo.i, hi.i)
  If mmu3_nc_ready <> 0 Or mmu3_ncN >= #MMU3_NC_MAX Or hi < lo Or lo < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  mmu3_nc_lo[mmu3_ncN] = lo
  mmu3_nc_hi[mmu3_ncN] = hi
  mmu3_ncN = mmu3_ncN + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuNcCount()
  ProcedureReturn mmu3_ncN
EndProcedure

; Return true only when the complete byte span is contained by one of the
; exact ranges registered before MmuBuildTables(). Mailbox callers use
; this to prove their buffer is Normal Non-Cacheable after M/C are enabled.
Procedure.i MmuIsNcRange(lo.i, hi.i)
  Protected i.i
  Protected pa.i
  Protected last.i
  If mmu3_nc_ready = 0 Or (MmuSctlr() & 5) <> 5 Or lo < 0 Or hi < lo Or hi >= #MMU3_PERIPH_BASE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To mmu3_ncN - 1
    If lo >= mmu3_nc_lo[i] And hi <= mmu3_nc_hi[i]
      pa = lo & (~$1FFFFF)
      last = hi & (~$1FFFFF)
      While pa <= last
        If MmuDescriptorL2Low(#MMU3_TABLE_BASE, pa >> 21) <> (pa | #MMU3_ATTR_NC)
          ProcedureReturn 0
        EndIf
        pa = pa + $200000
      Wend
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; Does the 2 MiB block starting at pa overlap any non-cacheable range?
Procedure.i mmu3_BlockNc(pa.i)
  Define i.i
  Define top.i
  top = pa + $1FFFFF
  For i = 0 To mmu3_ncN - 1
    If mmu3_nc_lo[i] <= top And mmu3_nc_hi[i] >= pa
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuBuildTables(base) - write the three tables. TOUCHES NO SYSTEM
;  REGISTER.
;
;  SEPARATE FROM MmuEnable() ON PURPOSE. This procedure only writes
;  DRAM, so it can be called and its output dumped and read word by word
;  with the MMU still off and the board in no danger at all. That is what
;  makes this stage provable: everything that could be wrong is
;  inspectable BEFORE the one instruction that commits to it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuBuildTables(base.i)
  Define i.i
  Define pa.i
  Define l2lo.i
  Define l2hi.i
  Define d.i

  If base = 0
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_NULL
  EndIf
  If (base & $FFF) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_ALIGN
  EndIf
  ; Below $1F00000 is the monitor's stack, its BSS, its code and the
  ; loader's staging arena. Tables written there would corrupt the thing
  ; that is about to translate through them.
  If base < $1F00000
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_LOW
  EndIf
  ; And they must not reach the loader's handoff page, which the A/B
  ; machinery reads back after the confirm.
  If base + #MMU3_TABLE_BYTES > $1FF0000
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_HIGH
  EndIf

  l2lo = base + 4096      ; splits level-1 slot 0, $00000000..$3FFFFFFF
  l2hi = base + 8192      ; splits level-1 slot 1, $40000000..$7FFFFFFF

  ; ---- level-2 for slot 0: DRAM, then the peripheral aperture -------
  For i = 0 To #MMU3_L2_ENTRIES - 1
    pa = i << 21
    If pa >= #MMU3_PERIPH_BASE
      d = pa | #MMU3_ATTR_DEVICE
    ElseIf mmu3_BlockNc(pa) <> 0
      d = pa | #MMU3_ATTR_NC
    Else
      d = pa | #MMU3_DRAM_ATTR
    EndIf
    ; PokeI, eight bytes. A descriptor is 64 bits and PokeL would write
    ; four, leaving the top half of every entry holding whatever DRAM
    ; happened to contain - which for a table this size is a walk into
    ; arbitrary memory.
    PokeI(l2lo + (i << 3), d)
  Next

  ; ---- level-2 for slot 1: the ARM-local window, then nothing -------
  ; Only the blocks that contain the 4 KB local window are mapped. The
  ; rest of the gigabyte FAULTS, which is the honest answer: there is no
  ; hardware there.
  For i = 0 To #MMU3_L2_ENTRIES - 1
    pa = #MMU3_LOCAL_BASE + (i << 21)
    If i < #MMU3_LOCAL_BLOCKS
      d = pa | #MMU3_ATTR_DEVICE
    Else
      d = #MMU3_DESC_FAULT
    EndIf
    PokeI(l2hi + (i << 3), d)
  Next

  ; ---- level 1: four slots -----------------------------------------
  For i = 0 To #MMU3_L1_ENTRIES - 1
    If i = 0
      d = l2lo | #MMU3_DESC_TABLE
    ElseIf i = 1
      d = l2hi | #MMU3_DESC_TABLE
    Else
      d = #MMU3_DESC_FAULT
    EndIf
    PokeI(base + (i << 3), d)
  Next

  ; Hand the enable sequence its three values here, not inside it, so
  ; that everything MmuEnable() needs is already in memory before it is
  ; called.
  mmu3_ttbr = base
  mmu3_tcr  = #MMU3_TCR
  mmu3_mair = #MMU3_MAIR

  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Read one descriptor back, so a probe or a gate can check the tables
;  with the MMU still off. "The table builder is correct" is a claim that
;  should be tested by something other than the table builder.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuDescriptor(base.i, slot.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekI(base + (slot << 3))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuDescriptorL2Low(base.i, slot.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekI(base + 4096 + (slot << 3))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuDescriptorL2High(base.i, slot.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekI(base + 8192 + (slot << 3))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuAttrFor(pa) - what attribute word a physical address SHOULD get.
;
;  THE ONE PURE PROCEDURE IN THIS FILE, and it exists for the same
;  reason HwMemFromRevision() exists in RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_mem.pi4:
;  it is the only part of the map a desk gate can EXECUTE. Everything
;  else here writes DRAM or a system register. A gate compiles this
;  procedure, runs the emitted A64 over the boundaries of THE MAP above
;  and over the addresses either side of each one, and rejects an
;  off-by-one, a swapped attribute and a missing access flag.
;
;  MmuBuildTables() and RaspberryPi3/Board/hw_addr.pi3 are both written
;  against it so that the tables and the guard cannot drift apart: there
;  is one statement of what is where, and both readers ask it.
;  It answers #MMU3_DESC_FAULT for an address with nothing behind it.
;
;  IT DOES NOT CONSULT THE NON-CACHEABLE LIST. Those are a run-time
;  decision about coherency, not a statement about what kind of thing is
;  at an address, and folding them in here would make the gate's
;  expectations depend on what a caller happened to register.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuAttrFor(pa.i)
  If pa < 0
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_DESC_FAULT
  EndIf
  If pa < #MMU3_PERIPH_BASE
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_DRAM_ATTR
  EndIf
  ; THE PERIPHERAL APERTURE AND THE PROCESSOR-LOCAL WINDOW ARE
  ; ADJACENT ON THIS PART: $3FFFFFFF + 1 IS $40000000. An earlier
  ; version of this procedure carried an arm refusing the gap between
  ; them, and the gate caught it as dead code on its first run - there
  ; is no gap, the arm could never be reached, and a refusal sentence
  ; nobody can ever see is a refusal nobody can act on.
  If pa <= #MMU3_PERIPH_TOP
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ATTR_DEVICE
  EndIf
  If pa <= #MMU3_LOCAL_TOP
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ATTR_DEVICE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #MMU3_DESC_FAULT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuBarrier() - dsb sy; isb. Its own procedure because several callers
;  outside this file need exactly this pair and an ASM block in a board
;  file is a place a register gets clobbered.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MmuBarrier()
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuEnabled() / MmuCachesOn() - what is actually on, read from the
;  register of the current level.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuEnabled()
  ProcedureReturn (MmuSctlr() & 1) <> 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MmuCachesOn()
  ProcedureReturn (MmuSctlr() & 4) <> 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuState() - the HwMmuState() answer: bit 0 MMU, bit 1 D-cache,
;  bit 2 I-cache. Note the bit ORDER is the seam's, not SCTLR's - the
;  seam numbers them 0,1,2 and SCTLR puts them at 0,2,12 - which is
;  exactly the kind of quiet mismatch that makes a status line wrong
;  without making it look wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuState()
  Define s.i
  Define r.i
  s = MmuSctlr()
  r = 0
  If (s & 1) <> 0
    r = r | 1
  EndIf
  If (s & 4) <> 0
    r = r | 2
  EndIf
  If (s & $1000) <> 0
    r = r | 4
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuEnable() - THE SWITCH. The single most dangerous sequence here.
;
;  THE TWO ARMS ARE DUPLICATED RATHER THAN FACTORED, and that is
;  deliberate. The obvious tidy-up is one helper with the register name
;  passed in, which this assembler cannot express: a system register is
;  part of the instruction, not an operand. The next tidy-up is a helper
;  per level called from the middle of the sequence, and that puts a
;  stack push and pop between the register loads and the enable. Two
;  copies of eighteen instructions is cheaper than debugging that.
;
;  THE VALUES ARE THE SAME AT BOTH LEVELS. TCR_EL3 and MAIR_EL3 have the
;  same field layout as their EL2 twins, both being the one-VA-range
;  no-VHE format, which is why there is no separate EL3 constant to
;  disagree with them.
;
;  M ONLY, NOT M|C|I. This procedure turns translation on and leaves the
;  caches alone, so the first thing tried on silicon separates "the
;  tables are wrong" from "the caches broke something".
;  MmuEnableCached() below is the one the monitor actually calls once
;  the tables have been read back on a board.
;
;  `orr x9, x9, x10` rather than an immediate: the assembler's logical
;  immediate encoding is a bitmask form this file does not need to rely
;  on, and a register is unambiguous.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MmuEnable()
  If MmuAtEl3() <> 0
    ASM
      dsb sy
      tlbi alle3
      dsb sy
      isb

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_ttbr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_ttbr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  ttbr0_el3, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_tcr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_tcr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  tcr_el3, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_mair
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_mair
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  mair_el3, x9

      dsb sy
      isb

      mrs  x9, sctlr_el3
      movz x10, #1
      orr  x9, x9, x10
      msr  sctlr_el3, x9
      isb
    EndASM
  Else
    ASM
      dsb sy
      tlbi alle2
      dsb sy
      isb

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_ttbr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_ttbr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  ttbr0_el2, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_tcr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_tcr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  tcr_el2, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_mair
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_mair
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  mair_el2, x9

      dsb sy
      isb

      mrs  x9, sctlr_el2
      movz x10, #1
      orr  x9, x9, x10
      msr  sctlr_el2, x9
      isb
    EndASM
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuEnableCached() - translation AND both caches, in one step.
;
;  ONE STEP AND NOT TWO, and the reason is specific to this board rather
;  than a preference. Between a bare MmuEnable() and a later "now set C
;  and I" there is a window in which translation is on and caching is
;  off; every byte the monitor writes in that window - its console ring,
;  its globals, the prompt it is about to print - is written
;  Non-Cacheable, and every byte it reads afterwards comes from a cache
;  that never saw them. Cleaning that up correctly means the very
;  cache-maintenance discipline this file is trying to avoid needing. So
;  the caches come on with the MMU, at a moment when nothing has been
;  written since the tables, or they do not come on at all.
;
;  The three ASM sequences differ from MmuEnable()'s only in the mask:
;  #MMU3_SCTLR_MCI is $1005, a single movz-able word.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure MmuEnableCached()
  If MmuAtEl3() <> 0
    ASM
      dsb sy
      tlbi alle3
      dsb sy
      isb

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_ttbr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_ttbr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  ttbr0_el3, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_tcr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_tcr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  tcr_el3, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_mair
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_mair
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  mair_el3, x9

      dsb sy
      isb

      mrs  x9, sctlr_el3
      movz x10, #4101
      orr  x9, x9, x10
      msr  sctlr_el3, x9
      isb
    EndASM
  Else
    ASM
      dsb sy
      tlbi alle2
      dsb sy
      isb

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_ttbr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_ttbr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  ttbr0_el2, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_tcr
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_tcr
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  tcr_el2, x9

      adrp x9, global_mmu3_mair
      add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_mmu3_mair
      ldr  x9, [x9]
      msr  mair_el2, x9

      dsb sy
      isb

      mrs  x9, sctlr_el2
      movz x10, #4101
      orr  x9, x9, x10
      msr  sctlr_el2, x9
      isb
    EndASM
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MmuUp() - build and switch, with the one check that matters between.
;
;  THE READ-BACK IS NOT DECORATION. MmuBuildTables() writes DRAM through
;  the same pointer machinery everything else uses, and a table that did
;  not land - a bad base, a range that did not take - produces a hang
;  with no console at the following instruction. Reading slot 0 back and
;  requiring it to be the table descriptor it was told to write costs
;  one load and turns that hang into a refusal that can be printed.
;
;  It answers 0 on refusal WITHOUT having touched a system register, so
;  the caller can say so on a console that still works.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MmuUp()
  Define rc.i
  mmu3_nc_ready = 0
  rc = MmuBuildTables(#MMU3_TABLE_BASE)
  If rc <> 0
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf
  If MmuDescriptor(#MMU3_TABLE_BASE, 0) <> ((#MMU3_TABLE_BASE + 4096) | #MMU3_DESC_TABLE)
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_NULL
  EndIf
  If MmuDescriptor(#MMU3_TABLE_BASE, 1) <> ((#MMU3_TABLE_BASE + 8192) | #MMU3_DESC_TABLE)
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_NULL
  EndIf
  If MmuDescriptor(#MMU3_TABLE_BASE, 3) <> #MMU3_DESC_FAULT
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_NULL
  EndIf
  MmuEnableCached()
  If (MmuSctlr() & 5) <> 5
    ProcedureReturn #MMU3_ERR_NULL
  EndIf
  mmu3_nc_ready = 1
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
